For a long time, people believed termite mounds kept the nest at a perfectly steady temperature, like a building with [[@air-conditioning|air conditioning]]. Careful measurements showed it is more complicated. Some nests stay fairly steady, while others warm and cool with the seasons.

In 2015, scientists studied mounds of an Indian termite near [[@bengaluru|Bengaluru]]. They placed [[sensor|sensors]] inside and found that the mounds have ridges, called flutes, which heat and cool faster than the centre. In the day, warm air rises through the flutes and sinks down the middle. At night, the flow turns around. The mounds were using the daily [[rhythm|rhythm]] of heat and cold to breathe.

