Train wheels and rails are both made of hard steel, and they touch over an area about the size of a small coin. There is very little rolling [[@friction|friction]], so a train needs far less energy to move a tonne of cargo than a truck does. One locomotive can pull a goods train more than a kilometre long.

Low [[friction|friction]] has a downside. Steel wheels can slip on wet rails or fallen leaves, and trains take a long time to stop. That's why railway signals warn drivers far ahead, and why level crossings have gates. A heavy goods train moving at full speed can need a kilometre or more to come to a stop.

