In Scratch, the free coding tool made at MIT, the stage is a grid 480 steps wide and 360 high. The centre is (0, 0). Move right and x goes up. Move up and y goes up. This is the same [[@coordinates|coordinate]] grid from maths class, with negative numbers for left and down.

To make a sprite walk, you add to its x. To make it jump, you add to its y, then subtract a bit more each moment to pull it back down. That is a simple model of [[gravity|gravity]]. Many game makers start exactly this way before learning the physics formulas behind real-looking motion.

