Ahmedabad's mosques mix Islamic, Hindu and Jain styles, because local craftsmen built them. The most famous carving is at the Sidi Saiyyed mosque, built in 1573. Its stone windows, called jali, show a tree with curling branches, cut from stone so finely that it looks like lace. The design has become a symbol of the city.

Near Ahmedabad is the [[@india-wonder-rani-ki-vav|Adalaj stepwell]], built in 1499. Steps lead several storeys down to cool water, past carved pillars and walls. Stepwells kept people supplied with water through the hot, dry months, and gave them a shady place to rest. Down at the bottom, the air can feel several degrees cooler than at the top.

