The rubber fig is a cousin of the [[@banyan|banyan]], and like the banyan it grows [[aerial-root|aerial roots]], roots that hang in the air before reaching the ground. Young roots are soft and bendy. Builders lay them along guides of bamboo or split areca palm stems laid across the stream, and gently tie them in place.

Where two roots press together, they slowly fuse into one. Scientists call this inosculation. Over decades, the roots form a thick net that can carry dozens of people at once. Some bridges stretch more than 20 m, longer than a cricket pitch.

