Krishna tells Arjuna that the true self, the soul, is never born and never dies. Bodies change, like clothes, but the self goes on. He urges Arjuna to do his duty, or dharma, as a warrior.

One of the best-known ideas is about action. Krishna says people should do their work well without being attached to the results, whether success or failure. He describes several paths to God: selfless action (karma yoga), knowledge (jnana yoga) and loving devotion ([[bhakti|bhakti]] yoga). Near the end, Krishna shows Arjuna a vast cosmic form, filling all of space and time.

{{story:mahabharata}}

Writing can be a big hurdle. Mandarin uses [[@chinese-characters|characters]], each standing for a word or part of a word. A reader needs to know a few thousand to read a newspaper. Japanese uses three scripts at once, including two sets of sound symbols and thousands of characters borrowed from Chinese.

Sounds matter too. Mandarin has tones: the [[syllable|syllable]] "ma" can mean mother, horse or to scold, depending on whether your voice rises, falls or dips. Some languages of southern Africa use click sounds. Arabic has sounds made deep in the throat that English speakers find tricky.

